Subject: [PATCH] Runtime: thread placement on processors, a setting (PS2X_THREAD_PLACEMENT, off by default)

ps2_thread_placement::place(role), called by each busy thread as it starts, gives it an affinity mask from the
processor topology (Windows; a no-op elsewhere). Adapted from another recompilation of this game, where placing its
EE and geometry threads on one L3 group and its GS threads on the other took a Ryzen 5 3600 from ~37 ms a frame,
unsteady, to a steady 33.3 ms. Here the threads are different, so the mapping is too:

- two or more L3 groups: EE, VIF1 and the native renderer's read thread (the threads that touch the frame in guest
  memory) on three cores of a group without core 0; the native render thread and the GS feed on the second and
  third cores of core 0's group;
- one L3: those five on cores 1 to 5;
- only the fast cores count on a CPU with performance and efficiency cores; fewer than 6, or more than one processor
  group: nothing placed;
- helpers (sound mixer, movie decoder, HD texture uploads and loaders, the controller thread): anywhere but the EE's
  and the render thread's cores.

PS2X_THREAD_PLACEMENT=off (the default) or unset makes no system call and changes nothing; =auto places. It is on the
Graphics page and takes a restart. PS2X_THREAD_CPUS=<ee>,<vif1>,<read>,<render>,<gsfeed> overrides auto's processor
for each, to try placements on a bench. Each placed thread logs "[threads] <role> thread -> processors 0x...".

This patch places the runtime's threads (game thread, VIF1, GS feed, Sony pad host); the port places its own
(native renderer, sound mixer, movie decoder, HD uploads) through the same call.

